=begin
  shard-origin.lic  (managed by Saga - it is rewritten on each Via-Lich launch)

  A back-compat shim for Lich builds older than 5.19. Saga launches Lich with
  --saga; Lich 5.19+ understands that flag and does EVERYTHING this script
  exists for natively: it labels this client Frontend.client == "saga",
  prefixes the origin sentinel to each downstream line (lib/games.rb, via the
  :sentinel frontend capability), treats Saga as a Wrayth-equivalent
  room_window client for room-number injection, and re-emits the playerID to
  each attaching client (lib/main/main.rb). On such a Lich, Saga does not
  auto-start this script at all, and if run anyway (manually, or via an old
  autostart entry) it detects the native support and exits immediately.

  On older Lich builds, which ignore --saga and label us "profanity", this
  script provides all three jobs:

  1. ORIGIN TAGGING. Prepends a sentinel byte (\x1f, the ASCII "unit separator")
     to the start of every game-server line on its way to the frontend, so Saga
     can tell real game text apart from Lich's own injected output (script echoes,
     "--- Lich:" status lines, lich5-update tables, etc.). Lich's OWN output does
     not pass through the downstream hook, so it arrives unmarked; Saga strips the
     sentinel and dims any unmarked main-stream line. \x1f is non-printing.

  2. FRONTEND NORMALIZATION. The detachable-client attach makes Lich label us
     'profanity' (Frontend.client), and Lich gates its room-number injection -
     "[Name - <lichid>] (u<uid>)" in the room/Story window - on a 'wrayth' /
     'stormfront' client (see lib/games.rb). Saga IS a Wrayth-XML client, so we
     re-assert Frontend.client = 'wrayth' here, on the downstream path that runs
     just before that injection, so room numbers reach Saga exactly as Wrayth.
     (This touches only Frontend.client; $frontend / GSL handling are untouched.)

  3. PLAYERID RE-EMIT (native since Lich 5.19: lib/main/main.rb re-emits per
     attach via Frontend.player_id_tag; before that, done here).
     When Saga attaches as a --detachable-client, Lich has ALREADY consumed the
     game's one-time <playerID id='...'/> during its own login handshake, so
     that tag never reaches Saga. Saga's cloud profile sync keys each character
     on its playerID, so without it a Via-Lich character silently never syncs
     (while the SAME character logged in Direct - e.g. on the web client - does,
     because it sees the tag). Lich stores the id verbatim
     (xmlparser.rb: @player_id = attributes['id']), so re-emitting
     <playerID id='#{XMLData.player_id}'/> reproduces exactly what a Direct
     login delivers, and both surfaces resolve the same sync key. We emit it
     once per attached client, so a Saga that RE-attaches mid-session (a
     reconnect after a socket blip, or a Saga restart against a surviving Lich)
     also relearns the id. The loop that watches for an attach polls briskly
     only until the first client is served, then settles to a lazy tick.

  Saga auto-starts this via --start-scripts on every Via-Lich login. You can
  also run it manually (;shard-origin) or add it to autostart.

  CAVEAT: if other Lich scripts' waitfor / matchwait / get stop matching game
  lines while this runs, the sentinel is leaking into the script line buffer on
  your Lich build - ;kill shard-origin and report back.
=end

# Lich 5.19+ launched with --saga does all three jobs natively; nothing below
# would do unique work (the loop would only duplicate main.rb's per-attach
# playerID emit). Saga skips --start-scripts on such a Lich, so this guard only
# fires for manual runs and old autostart entries. Feature-detect instead of a
# version compare: Frontend.player_id_tag shipped in the same Lich release as
# the rest of the native support, so its presence (with the saga label, proof
# the running Lich actually parsed --saga) means all of it is present.
if defined?(Frontend) && Frontend.client.eql?('saga') && Frontend.respond_to?(:player_id_tag)
  exit
end

# JOB 3 (pre-5.19 Lich): re-emit the <playerID> tag to the Saga frontend.
# Lich consumed the game's one-time <playerID> during its own login handshake
# before Saga's detachable-client attached, so Saga never sees it - and Saga's
# cloud profile sync keys on the playerID, so without this a Via-Lich character
# silently never syncs. XMLData.player_id holds the id verbatim, so this
# reproduces exactly what a Direct login delivers. _respond routes to the
# detachable client (global_defs.rb) and gates on XMLData.safe_to_respond?.
# The emit itself happens in the resident loop below, once per attached client
# (Saga usually attaches AFTER this script starts, so a startup-only emit would
# race ahead of the attach and _respond would drop it against a not-yet-alive
# client).
def saga_player_id
  (defined?(XMLData) ? XMLData.player_id.to_s : '')
end

# The live detachable client, or nil when none is attached. Every attach wraps a
# freshly accepted socket in a NEW SynchronizedSocket (lib/main/main.rb), and
# alive? is a one-way latch (a dead socket never revives), so the object itself
# identifies one attach: a client we have already served can never come back,
# and a re-attach is always a different object. The loop keys on that instead of
# on an alive/not-alive EDGE, which a lazy poll could straddle and miss.
def saga_detachable_client
  return nil unless defined?($_DETACHABLE_CLIENT_) && $_DETACHABLE_CLIENT_
  $_DETACHABLE_CLIENT_.alive? ? $_DETACHABLE_CLIENT_ : nil
end

def saga_emit_player_id
  pid = saga_player_id
  _respond("<playerID id='#{pid}'/>") if pid =~ /\A\d+\z/
end

# On a --saga-aware Lich the sentinel + frontend normalization happen natively
# in lib/games.rb, so skip our now-redundant DownstreamHook. Older Lich builds
# still label us 'profanity' and rely on the hook below.
unless defined?(Frontend) && Frontend.client.eql?('saga')
  DownstreamHook.add('shard-origin', proc { |server_string|
    # (A) Frontend label: the detachable-client attach makes Lich call us
    # 'profanity'; Lich gates its MOVEMENT room-number injection on a
    # wrayth/stormfront client (lib/games.rb). Saga speaks Wrayth XML, so say so.
    Frontend.client = 'wrayth' if defined?(Frontend) && Frontend.client != 'wrayth'

    # (B) Origin sentinel: prefix \x1f to the start of each line so Saga can tell
    # genuine game output from Lich's own injected lines - EXCEPT the room-name
    # line. Lich injects the room number by matching that line with a ^-anchored
    # regex (process_room_information, lib/games.rb); a leading sentinel breaks the
    # match and suppresses the number. Mirror Lich's own anchor so we skip exactly
    # that line and tag every other line as before.
    roomname = /\A(?:<pushStream id="familiar"[^>]*\/>)?(?:<resource picture="\d+"\/>|<popBold\/>)?<style id="roomName"/
    server_string.gsub(/^.+$/) { |line| line =~ roomname ? line : "\x1f" + line }
  })

  before_dying { DownstreamHook.remove('shard-origin') }
end

# Stay resident and emit the playerID once per attached client, as soon as the id
# is known. This lands the tag inside Saga's post-attach wait for it
# (waitForPlayerId, 15s) and covers every LATER attach too: Saga re-attaches to a
# surviving Lich after a socket blip (LichTransport.startReconnect, retrying for
# 30s), and each attach resets Saga's playerId, so a session handed the tag only
# once silently drops to local-only settings after its first reconnect. That is
# why this cannot simply exit after the first emit. On a pre---saga Lich the loop
# is also what keeps the DownstreamHook above registered, since before_dying
# strips it on exit.
#
# Only the FIRST attach is latency-sensitive (it holds Saga's sign-in splash
# while Saga waits for the tag), so poll briskly until one client has been served
# - time-bounded, so a Lich nobody ever attaches to settles down anyway - then
# tick lazily. A re-attach only has to be noticed inside Saga's 15s wait.
saga_served = nil
saga_brisk_until = Time.now + 60
loop do
  client = saga_detachable_client
  if client && client.object_id != saga_served && saga_player_id =~ /\A\d+\z/
    saga_emit_player_id
    saga_served = client.object_id
  end
  sleep(saga_served.nil? && Time.now < saga_brisk_until ? 0.2 : 2)
end
